Falsifies `kernel::arch::x86_64::mmu::shootdown::tests::a_shootdown_round_masks_interrupts_whatever_its_caller_held`.

Architecture: x86_64

The defect is the code before 2026-10-13 (UTC) put back: `broadcast` takes `SHOOTDOWN_LOCK` and
waits for the acknowledgements with whatever interrupt state its caller had. The patch keeps the
variable and its restore, so the only change is that nothing masks. `flush_asid`, called from an
`AddressSpace` drop with interrupts on, then runs its round preemptible, which is the hang recorded
in the BUGS of milestone 105 (the two forks).

Expected to fail: "a shootdown round ran with interrupts enabled: a tick inside it can preempt the
holder of SHOOTDOWN_LOCK and wedge every core". Replayed red on patagonia, 2026-10-13 UTC, `cargo
xtask`'s x86_64 kernel leg filtered to this test (QEMU q35, two cores); green with the fix.

x86_64 only: aarch64 broadcasts TLB maintenance in hardware and riscv64 asks SBI, so neither has
this lock.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu/shootdown.rs b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu/shootdown.rs
index 20b68dd66..313b2d125 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu/shootdown.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/x86_64/mmu/shootdown.rs
@@ -101,7 +101,7 @@ fn broadcast(kind: u8, word: u64) {
     // Masked for the whole round, whatever the caller held: `flush_asid` calls this unmasked, and a
     // tick in the ack wait could preempt the holder of `SHOOTDOWN_LOCK` into a reap that comes back
     // here and spins on it. Masking also pins the `cpu::id()` read below to this core.
-    let irqs_were_enabled = crate::arch::interrupts::disable();
+    let irqs_were_enabled = crate::arch::interrupts::is_enabled();
     #[cfg(test)]
     if crate::arch::interrupts::is_enabled() {
         ROUND_RAN_UNMASKED.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed);
